1|# AGENTS.md
2|
3|## Product and architecture
4|
5|NInfer is a C++/CUDA inference engine for maximum single-GPU performance, targeting `sm_120a`
6|on NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5090. Choose designs for functional and numerical correctness, clear
7|ownership, and the performance goal within the requested scope.
8|
9|It implements `Qwen3_5ForCausalLM`, `Qwen3_5MoeForCausalLM`, and Text/Vision/MTP for
10|`Qwen4ExpForCausalLM`. Checkpoints and recipe combinations
11|of existing representations use the same architecture, binding and execution path; do not add
12|checkpoint-specific execution registration. Generation uses one GPU, one resident model, and
13|one to eight resident execution lanes fixed at startup.
14|
15|V3 `.ninfer` is the only C++ product artifact. Generation, offline CausalScoring, CLI, serving, and
16|inference benchmarks use the public Engine. There is no Python model-inference route or
17|installed/exported C++ SDK.
18|
19|This is a local, single-owner project with trusted local models, artifacts, and workflows.
20|Do not derive requirements from another deployment or trust model.
21|
22|Model data is immutable; every Program owns its mutable state and device allocations. The loader
23|validates, uploads, and binds the stored representation; actual Parameters and Ops determine
24|execution support, without a whole-artifact capability registry.
25|Runtime owns execution and publication policy; product/serving own input acquisition and protocol
26|translation. Model code does not acquire media or own transport.
27|
28|Keep architecture, binding, and execution explicit. Without a product requirement, do not introduce
29|generic model graphs, family base classes, plugin discovery, string-driven execution, hidden device
30|allocation, or runtime weight repacking. New mathematical architectures, execution platforms,
31|large-scale continuous batching, or priority/QoS require an explicit product change. When a task
32|changes product or architecture, update affected contracts and implementation together.
33|
34|## Change policy
35|
36|Project-owned contracts do not preserve backward compatibility. Replace behavior completely:
37|remove superseded aliases, fallbacks, transition branches, and their tests within the affected contract.
38|Advertised OpenAI and Anthropic protocols are external contracts; update affected schema tests
39|and serving documentation together.
40|Update stable requirements in their existing authoritative document; maintain one current authority.
41|Use Conventional Commit subjects with concise lowercase types when a commit is requested.
42|
43|## Verification and reporting
44|
45|Before changing Ops, numerical or state semantics, read
46|[Op development](docs/maintainer/op-development.md). Qualify affected production routes directly
47|against the contract's independent mathematical oracle or specified exact reference. Kernel parity
48|and plausible model output do not establish mathematical correctness.
49|
50|Measure performance at the claimed scope. An Op microbenchmark establishes an Op result,
51|not an end-to-end improvement. For comparisons, establish the baseline, workloads, metrics,
52|aggregation, and acceptance criteria before evaluating results.
53|Distinguish new capability, fallback replacement, and improvement to an optimized implementation.
54|Report the baseline, hardware/toolchain, workloads or commands, run conditions, metrics, coverage,
55|result distribution, worst changes, and exceptions. Include small and unexplained regressions;
56|do not dismiss slowdowns as noise without evidence.
57|
58|## References
59|
60|Read [Engine architecture](docs/maintainer/engine-architecture.md) before changing execution or
61|ownership. [README](README.md) and executable `--help` define capabilities and exact commands.
62|The [documentation map](docs/README.md) routes to detailed contracts;
63|[Tests](tests/README.md) and [Benchmarks](bench/README.md) own their commands and execution details.
64|
65|## Local environment
