Round 5: implement T=1 → GEMV in launch_q4_range / launch_q5_range.

Current code (small.cu):
```cpp
void launch_q4_range(const Tensor& x, const Weight& w, std::int32_t row_begin, std::int32_t rows,
                     const Tensor& out, cudaStream_t stream) {
    const Weight view = sub_weight(w, row_begin, rows);
    const auto   dst  = LinearBf16StridedOutput{...};
    // [9B 验证] 强制走 SIMT（T=1 也支持），排除 gemv 的 sub_weight 偏移问题
    if (x.ne[1] <= 4) { launch_q4_a16_simt<SmallR8T4>(...); return; }
    if (x.ne[1] <= 8) { launch_q4_a16_simt<SmallR8T8>(...); return; }
    if (x.ne[1] <= 16) { sliced_k_mma<SlicedK4096T16> }
    ...
}
```
and
```cpp
void launch_q5_range(...) {
    // [9B 验证] 强制走 SIMT ...
    using SimtSchedule = Q5A16SimtSchedule<1, 4, 4, 16, 2, Cache::ca, 1>;
    launch_q5_a16_simt<SimtSchedule>(q5_linear_operands(x, view), dst, ...);
}
```

Change:
- q4: `if (x.ne[1] == 1) { launch_q4_gemv(x, view, out, stream); return; }` — but `launch_q4_gemv` takes `out` Tensor and builds its own dst. Actually existing `launch_q4_gemv(const Tensor& x, const Weight& weight, const Tensor& out, cudaStream_t)` uses `q4_linear_operands(x, weight)` and out. Since `out` here is the range target tensor, passing `out` works (it uses out.ne[1] stride).
  Simplest: build a temporary: `launch_q4_gemv(x, view, out, stream); return;`

- q5: `if (x.ne[1] == 1) { launch_q5_gemv(x, view, out, stream); return; }` — q5_gemv signature: `launch_q5_gemv(const Tensor& x, const Weight& weight, const Tensor& value, const Tensor& z, stream)` — it's a **split2** output (value+z, kValueRows apart). But in launch_q5_range we write a single segment into `out`. So q5_gemv isn't directly usable.

For q5, the "range" call writes either gate(4096) or value(1024) into `out`. The gemv variant `launch_q5_a16_gemv<GemvR16W1G16S2XK4096>` writes 2 segments (value & z). Hmm, in launch_small: gate→out[2], value→out[3]. The q5 weight is 5120 rows = gate(4096)+value(1024)? Actually ordering: second weight rows 0..4095 = gate, 4096..5119 = value. And q5_projection_output(first=value, second=z) writes kValueRows apart...

Risky. Safer approach for this round: only optimize q4 T=1 → GEMV (q/k paths: query 4096 + key 1024 = both q4). And for q5 keep SIMT (gate 4096 + value 1024). q4 covers 5120 rows out of 10240; q5 the rest. So half the projection.

Hmm, limited gain (~?). Actually the projection is only part of the cost; MLP (gate_up 34816 rows) dominates! For decode, MLP gate_up is n=34816,k=4096 → the big one. Which path does it take for T=1? Let me check `swiglu/q4/shapes/n34816_k5120/dispatch.cpp` select_plan — it likely picks a GEMV for T=1 already (that's the main matmul).

Given budget, the biggest lever might be elsewhere. Let me measure instead of guessing: use nvtx? Simpler: the engine has `--request-log-jsonl` and `--log-stats-interval-ms`; maybe it logs per-op timings? The CLI `ninfer-bench` exists (bench/inference/ninfer_bench.cpp) but that's a separate build target.

Alternative quick measurement: compare tok/s with different max_context/KV dtype? No.

Practical: use Nsight? Not installed likely.

Cheapest real signal: run the model with `--no-cuda-graph` vs graph to see launch overhead share: 15.3 vs 36.2 → launches were ~58% of time. Now 36.2 tok/s = 27.6 ms/token. If we're at 226 GB/s, the memory-bound floor should be ~6.24GB/768GB/s = 8.1 ms → 123 tok/s. So there's 3.4× overhead beyond pure bandwidth.

Given limited context, the most informative cheap probe: does the 9B MLP (n=34816, k=4096) use GEMV at T=1? Read `select_plan` in that dispatch.cpp (the file I edited) — lines 1-80.

Let me read it. If MLP T=1 uses a slow path, that's the lever.

I'll read lines 1-82 of that file.

